WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.060
Chapter one asked what a label can prove.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.820
How can we test what a gene does in one cell type, after birth?

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:14.800
We need to control three things: the target, the place, and the time.

4
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:20.153
First, build the target.

5
00:00:21.553 --> 00:00:24.513
Place loxP sites around an important gene segment.

6
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:31.033
This floxed allele is designed to work normally until Cre recombinase removes that

7
00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:36.713
segment. A Cre-free control must test whether the allele really does work normally.

8
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:39.393
Next, control the place.

9
00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:44.753
A cell-type promoter supplies Cre only where that driver is active.

10
00:00:46.173 --> 00:00:49.313
Cre deletes the bracketed segment largely in those cells.

11
00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:55.533
But a driver's actual range and completeness must be measured, not assumed from its

12
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:56.533
name.

13
00:00:56.883 --> 00:00:58.423
Then control the time.

14
00:00:59.943 --> 00:01:04.783
CreER is designed to keep Cre outside the nucleus until tamoxifen is given.

15
00:01:06.043 --> 00:01:08.123
A gold time gate opens after birth.

16
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:14.703
Recombination is concentrated after dosing, but its actual timing must be measured.

17
00:01:15.817 --> 00:01:18.357
Now read the cross one locus at a time.

18
00:01:19.777 --> 00:01:22.677
AvilCre/+ supplies one Cre driver allele.

19
00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:28.777
TrkAfl/− supplies one floxed allele and one null allele.

20
00:01:30.037 --> 00:01:33.197
The semicolon joins the two instructions in one mouse.

21
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.947
A matched control changes one causal piece.

22
00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:41.667
Keep the floxed and null TrkA alleles, but remove Cre.

23
00:01:42.947 --> 00:01:46.767
That mouse preserves the target genotype without triggering deletion.

24
00:01:48.127 --> 00:01:52.627
A second control can retain Cre while keeping one working TrkA copy.

25
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:55.247
Now predict carefully.

26
00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:59.187
A cell glows green from a Cre-dependent reporter.

27
00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:03.707
Does that glow prove the separate floxed target gene was deleted?

28
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:16.383
No. The reporter and target are separate lox-flanked loci, and they can recombine

29
00:02:16.383 --> 00:02:21.403
differently. Old target protein can also persist after deletion.

30
00:02:23.203 --> 00:02:25.523
Reporter light shows reporter recombination.

31
00:02:26.463 --> 00:02:29.223
Measure the target protein itself in those cells.

32
00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:32.307
Only then interpret the outcome.

33
00:02:33.647 --> 00:02:37.687
Removing a gene tests whether it is necessary under those conditions.

34
00:02:39.087 --> 00:02:43.587
Imposing activity with an actuator tests whether activity is sufficient.

35
00:02:44.807 --> 00:02:48.407
A reporter observes a state; it does not impose that state.

36
00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:51.727
A conditional experiment is a chain.

37
00:02:53.007 --> 00:02:57.247
Match the genotype to the perturbation, the perturbation to the measured readout,

38
00:02:57.587 --> 00:02:58.767
and the readout to the claim.

39
00:03:00.147 --> 00:03:02.267
Check every link with a control in practice.

40
00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.427
One weak link limits the conclusion.
